A||||3||Oreif||Need help on bolt problem!||||9||
Z||000000||Oreif||02-16-2000||11:05 AM||||Hey all,<BR>I am replacing the front sway bar and the 2 rear bolts broke on the mounts. I drilled out the center and tried an Easy-Out. The #%%@$ thing broke. I am just going to drill them out and put in bolts thru the access panel in the frame, BUT Does anyone have an idea on how to drill out or remove the one with the broken Easy-out in it?  ||||reg||9
Z||000001||Phaeton||02-16-2000||11:15 AM||||Been there, atempted that.  I took a small (16th) drill bit and drilled down alongside the ezout.  the ezout is tapered and once I had drilled along three sides it started to wiggle and worked out.  Total time was three hours, but this was and trans mount hole on an engine block, and then the hole had to be drilled round again and a heli-coil installed.  I do have to agree with your #%%@$ opinion of the matter.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||02-16-2000||02:43 PM||||Heat makes a HUGE difference in removing rusted and/or broken bolts. I finally broke down and bought a small propane/oxygen torch when I broke a bolt holding the steering rack on. After heating it cherry red it came right out using vise grips on the VERY small part left exposed. It was barely finger tight.<P>The one I bought was on sale for $30 (normally $40). You can get just propane ones cheaper but they don't get nearly as hot. Also, with oxygen you can do some cutting and brazing. I have used it several times since. It is one of the best tool investments I have made.<BR> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||02-16-2000||03:14 PM||||I believe that the nut is spot welded to the frame and can be knocked out with a pointed chisal and a few well placed hammer strikes.||||reg||1
